  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule has already run once without errors (status `ok`). I couldn't check directly that its 5xx query matches real documents, because Kibana's console proxy is disabled here.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` has one document per web request, timed by the `timestamp` field. Each request records:\n- the visitor's IP address (`clientip`)\n- the URL and file extension requested\n- the response code (`response`, stored as text, with a `response.keyword` version for exact matching)\n- how many bytes were sent (`bytes`)\n- the visitor's browser (`agent`) and operating system (`machine.os`)\n- source and destination countries (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`) and map coordinates\n- the referring page and tags\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-1586864f Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-1586864f-web-logs-overview-32b7946c8bb4`, default view is the last 7 days) has 7 panels:\n- Single numbers for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code\n- A pie chart of response codes\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and bytes\n- A bar chart of requests by source country\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-1586864f 5xx spike\"** (id `f1d63047-e32d-49c3-a3e9-6e7b0a7e4f1d`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code is from 500 to 599.\n- It fires when more than 10 of them arrive within 5 minutes.\n- It is switched on and tagged `EFF-1586864f` and `web-logs`.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana; it won't send any notification. Adding a connector (for example Slack or email) would fix that.",
